# 1926. Nearest Exit from Entrance in Maze

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/nearest-exit-from-entrance-in-maze/
- **Topic:** Graphs, BFS, Shortest Path, Grid Traversal
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Solved

## 1. My Intuition

We can solve this problem using BFS starting from the entrance.

We need to explore all four directions: up, down, left, and right.

If we encounter an empty cell at the boundary of the maze, we have found an exit.

Since BFS explores cells level by level, the first exit we encounter gives the shortest distance.

The entrance itself should not be considered an exit.

## 2. My Approach

1. Get the number of rows and columns.
2. Add the entrance coordinates to the queue.
3. Mark the entrance as visited by changing it to '+'.
4. Perform BFS level by level.
5. For every cell removed from the queue:
   - Explore all four directions.
   - Skip cells that are outside the maze or contain '+'.
   - If a valid neighboring cell is on the boundary, return the current level count.
   - Otherwise, mark it visited and add it to the queue.
6. If BFS finishes without finding an exit, return -1.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

The main boundary condition needs careful attention.

A cell is on the boundary if:

r == 0 || r == m - 1 || c == 0 || c == n - 1

Here:
- r is compared with the number of rows (m).
- c is compared with the number of columns (n).

Also, the entrance must not be considered an exit, so we check neighboring cells rather than treating the initial entrance as an exit.

## 4. What Clicked

BFS is suitable because every movement has the same cost: one step.

Processing the queue level by level allows us to identify the nearest exit without exploring unnecessary levels.

Marking a cell as visited when adding it to the queue prevents repeated visits.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

class Solution {
    public int nearestExit(char[][] maze, int[] entrance) {
        int m = maze.length;
        int n = maze[0].length;

        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();

        q.offer(new int[]{entrance[0], entrance[1]});
        maze[entrance[0]][entrance[1]] = '+';

        int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        int count = 0;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();
            count++;

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                int[] curr = q.poll();

                int r = curr[0];
                int c = curr[1];

                for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
                    int nr = r + dr[k];
                    int nc = c + dc[k];

                    if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n ||
                        maze[nr][nc] == '+') {
                        continue;
                    }

                    if (nr == 0 || nr == m - 1 || nc == 0 || nc == n - 1) {
                        return count;
                    }

                    maze[nr][nc] = '+';
                    q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}

## 6. Complexity Analysis

Let m be the number of rows and n be the number of columns.

- **Time Complexity:** O(m × n)
- **Space Complexity:** O(m × n)

Each cell is visited at most once, and the queue can contain up to O(m × n) cells.

